<p>Every computation begins with Section 12: the day of judgment is excluded, and the time requisite for obtaining the certified copy is excluded — provided the copy was applied for. An appellant who never applied for the copy loses the exclusion, which alone sinks many borderline appeals.</p>

<h2>What counts as sufficient cause</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><h3>Custody and communication</h3><p>The convict lodged in jail, dependent on jail visits and legal aid for every step — the paradigmatic sufficient cause, especially where the jail appeal machinery itself moved slowly.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h3>Indigence and legal aid</h3><p>Time consumed in obtaining legal aid counsel, records and copies through DSLSA channels is regularly accepted when documented.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h3>Illness and family circumstance</h3><p>Genuine, documented incapacity of the appellant or the family member prosecuting the appeal — with medical records, not adjectives.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h3>Counsel\'s default</h3><p>Failure of previous counsel to file or inform, where the litigant acted diligently on discovery, is a recognised ground; courts distinguish the diligent client from the indifferent one.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>The anatomy of a persuasive application</h2>
<p>Weak applications share a form: two paragraphs, one adjective (&ldquo;bona fide&rdquo;), no dates. Strong applications share a different form: a table. Day-by-day or period-by-period, from the judgment to the filing: when the copy was applied for and received (with endorsements), when the family learned of the outcome, when legal aid was sought and granted, when the file reached counsel, what happened in each interval. Supporting documents — the copying agency endorsements, jail communication records, medical papers — are annexed against each period. The affidavit is sworn by the person with knowledge of each fact, not by a clerk reciting instructions. An application built this way converts condonation from mercy into entitlement: the cause is proved, not pleaded.</p>

<h2>Opposing condonation: when and how</h2>
<p>For respondents — the State opposing a late convict\'s appeal rarely profits, but complainants and victims opposing late acquittal-appeals or the State opposing stale revisions have real interests. The opposition that works is forensic, not rhetorical: take the application\'s own chronology and expose its gaps — the unexplained ninety days between copy and counsel, the copy never applied for, the earlier proceeding proving knowledge of the judgment. Argue prejudice concretely where it exists: witnesses relocated, the acquitted citizen\'s rebuilt life, evidence dissipated. And remember the asymmetry courts apply — the latitude extended to a prisoner is not a template for the State\'s file-notings, and submissions should hold each applicant to the standard the case law sets for that class of applicant.</p>

<div class="check">
<ul>
  <li><strong>Apply for the certified copy the day of judgment</strong> — Section 12\'s exclusion is earned, not presumed.</li>
  <li><strong>File appeal, condonation application and suspension/bail application as one set.</strong></li>
  <li><strong>Explain every interval with a document;</strong> unexplained gaps, not total length, kill applications.</li>
  <li><strong>On discovery of previous counsel\'s default, act within days</strong> and say so on affidavit.</li>
</ul>
</div>

<div class="note">
<p><strong>Practice note:</strong> Condonation restores the appeal, not the lost time\'s consequences — the sentence may have been running throughout. Where delay is anticipated (records in transit, sanction pending), consider filing within time with available documents and seeking leave to supplement, rather than perfecting a late appeal. A timely imperfect filing nearly always beats a perfect late one.</p>
</div>
